# Test: ze config validate REFUSES an mcp block whose second server names no port
# VALIDATES: the operator-visible half of the missing-port guard. ExtractMCPConfig
# tested only Servers[0], so this exact config returned ok=true, entry `alt`
# reached the binder as "127.0.0.1:" and the kernel chose the port -- an MCP
# listener on a port nobody asked for and no doctor check probes. It now starts
# NO listener, and refusing in silence would be the same failure wearing a
# different hat, so validation has to say which entry and what to write.
# PREVENTS: a partial mcp listener set starting, and the refusal being silent.
#
# Sibling: mcp-multi-listener.ci proves two COMPLETE entries are accepted, which
# is what makes this file's refusal meaningful rather than a blanket rejection of
# multiple entries.
#
# One command on purpose: expect=stdout:contains= matches the file's ACCUMULATED
# stdout, so a second command's output can satisfy the first's assertions.
stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	router-id 10.0.0.1;
	session { asn { local 65533; } }
}
environment {
	mcp {
		enabled true;
		server primary {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port 8080;
		}
		server alt {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze config validate -:stdin=config
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stdout:contains=configuration invalid
expect=stdout:contains=server alt
expect=stdout:contains=names no port
# The shipped YANG declares `refine port { default 8080; }` and the daemon never
# applies it, so the message names that divergence instead of claiming mcp has no
# default. An operator who followed the refine is exactly who lands here.
expect=stdout:contains=8080
